.TH fetchloom 1  fetchloom "fetchloom manual"
.SH NAME
fetchloom \- put the exact data a project needs onto any machine with one command
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfetchloom\fR [options] <command>
.SH DESCRIPTION
fetchloom puts the exact research data a project needs into its data/ folder.
.PP
  data.toml  the datasets you want
  pinning    recording a dataset\*(Aqs exact files and their SHA\-256 in data.lock
  data.lock  the list of pinned files, so every machine gets the same bytes
  the cache  one folder that keeps each file once, for all your projects
.PP
  fetchloom add iris    choose an iris dataset, put it in data/ and pin it
  fetchloom get iris    download it once, without a project
  fetchloom sync        fill data/ in a project you cloned
.PP
a name is looked up on hugging face, kaggle, zenodo and more. a link works too:
  fetchloom add https://zenodo.org/records/3608990
.PP
learn more: `fetchloom \-\-help` lists the commands;
`fetchloom help <topic>` explains concepts, recipes, environment or exit\-codes
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
print only the answer, warnings and errors
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
say more: \-v adds detail and counters, \-vv adds debug logs
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
print one JSON document on stdout, not the usual output
.TP
\fB\-\-progress\fR
with \-\-json, keep plain progress lines on stderr
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
use only what is already on this machine
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
answer yes to questions, but never pick a dataset for you
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR \fI<when>\fR
when to use color: auto, the default, always or never
.TP
\fB\-C\fR \fI<dir>\fR
run as if fetchloom started in <dir>
.TP
\fB\-\-mirror\fR \fI<url>\fR
ask the mirror at this web address or folder for files before their sources. repeat it for more
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-config\fR
leave the settings file unread for this run
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
print help: \-h the common part, \-\-help all of it
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
print the version
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBadd\fR
add a dataset to the project and download it into the data folder
.TP
\fBremove\fR
remove a dataset and the unchanged files fetchloom placed for it
.TP
\fBdrop\fR
leave files out of their dataset for good, such as bad samples
.TP
\fBsync\fR
put the files data.lock lists into the data folder
.TP
\fBlock\fR
update data.lock from data.toml, without changing the data folder
.TP
\fBbuild\fR
run the recipes and record their results in data.lock
.TP
\fBstatus\fR
list files that are missing, changed locally or extra
.TP
\fBlist\fR
list the project's datasets: source, size and folder
.TP
\fBexport\fR
write the SHA\-256 or link of each file, for other tools
.TP
\fBpush\fR
copy the files data.lock lists into a folder mirror
.TP
\fBpath\fR
print where a dataset or a file in it is, syncing it if needed
.TP
\fBcache\fR
show the cache, free what no project needs, or verify it
.TP
\fBsettings\fR
show each setting, its value and where it came from
.TP
\fBget\fR
fetch something once, without a project
.TP
\fBsearch\fR
find datasets on hugging face, kaggle, zenodo and other sites
.TP
\fBauth\fR
sign in for private data, or data with terms to accept
.TP
\fBcompletions\fR
print a script that completes fetchloom's commands in a shell
.TP
\fBhelp\fR
explain the ideas behind fetchloom, or one command
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBfetchloom <command> \-\-help\fR lists what a command takes, and \fBfetchloom help <topic>\fR explains concepts, recipes, the environment and exit codes. the guide is at https://nikhil\-yn\-aix.github.io/fetchloom/
